WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.135 size:84% position:42%
-Our next guest is a
Grammy-winning musician

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:03.069 line:92%
and front man of the band Wilco.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.371 align:start size:94% position:6%
His book,
"How to Write One Song,"

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:07.640 align:start size:94% position:6%
and his latest solo album,
"Love Is the King,"

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:08.842 size:84% position:42% line:92%
are both available now.

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:11.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
Please welcome back to the sho
our friend Jeff Tweedy.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.546 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
How are you, Jeff?

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:14.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm good. How are you?
Good to see you.

00:00:14.781 --> 00:00:17.250 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm doing very well.
We've talked in the past.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:21.254 align:start size:94% position:6%
As you know, I'm a longtime fa
of yours and of Wilco,

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:22.889 line:92%
and I had this great honor

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:25.792 align:start size:94% position:6%
You asked a bunch of friends
of yours to be

00:00:25.792 --> 00:00:28.028 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the video for your new song
"Gwendolyn,"

00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:31.131 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I was lucky enough to be
one of those people.

00:00:31.131 --> 00:00:32.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
How did you come up
with this idea?

00:00:32.732 --> 00:00:33.233 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Here I am.

00:00:33.233 --> 00:00:35.869 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's basically the bottom half
of my face,

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:36.569 size:81% position:41% line:92%
the top half of yours.

00:00:36.569 --> 00:00:39.706 align:start size:94% position:6%
And how did you come up
with the idea?

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:45.078 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I saw a post on social medi
from Steve Martin,

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:47.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
where he was joking
that nobody recog--

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:49.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
Something was weird
about going out in public,

00:00:49.749 --> 00:00:53.553 align:start size:94% position:6%
so he had made a sign to wea
over his head that said,

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:55.755 size:84% position:42% line:92%
"This is Steve Martin."

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.758
And it made me think that I have
a lot of friends that

00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:04.097
are sort of celebrities,
and they don't get to use

00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:06.599 align:start size:94% position:6%
that part of their face
very much anymore.

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:09.769 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I thought maybe I could tak
advantage of that

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:14.074 align:start size:94% position:6%
and get everybody to donat
their face to my video.

00:01:14.074 --> 00:01:19.712 align:start size:94% position:6%
Plus, just masks just work
really well as a green screen,

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:23.883 align:start size:94% position:6%
so I just thought I'd take
advantage of our moment

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:24.417 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
in that way.

00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:27.487
-Well, I really appreciate being
part of that moment.

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:29.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
You recorded this album
with your family,

00:01:29.856 --> 00:01:33.927
and I imagine, at this point
Wilco must feel like famil

00:01:33.927 --> 00:01:36.696
when you record songs,
but it must also still be

00:01:36.696 --> 00:01:38.965 align:start size:94% position:6%
so different to do
with your actual family.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:39.933 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
What's that like?

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:43.770 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It's -- Playing music
with your family

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:49.242
is just the most amazing gif
I can ever imagine being given

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:54.514 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's -- You know, music relies
upon trust and, you know,

00:01:54.514 --> 00:01:58.818
just faith that you're going t
be there in a supportive rol

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:06.759
for each other, and musician
work long -- I don't know.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:09.062 align:start size:94% position:6%
They work for
a long time, usually.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:11.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
A band has to be together
for years

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:15.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
to develop all of those
types of trusts.

00:02:15.268 --> 00:02:19.038 align:start size:94% position:6%
And a family just kind of
has it as a --

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:23.810 align:start size:94% position:6%
starting point, a base, yo
know, level of trust that is -

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:24.377 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
I don't know.

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:26.779
It just feels like you just have
so much more room

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:29.616 line:92%
to get intimate from there

00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:33.219 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it's the best
thing I can think of to do

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:33.987 line:92%
-Hey, congrats on the book

00:02:33.987 --> 00:02:38.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is basically -- You'r
trying to give people advice o

00:02:38.825 --> 00:02:39.859 size:81% position:41% line:92%
how to write one song.

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:44.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
And one of the things that
you sort of stress is

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:48.568 align:start size:94% position:6%
how important it is to
let inspiration in,

00:02:48.568 --> 00:02:50.103 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
to invite it in.

00:02:50.103 --> 00:02:51.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
Can you explain
that a little bit?

00:02:51.738 --> 00:03:02.415
-Well, everybody, I think,
imagines that artistic processes

00:03:02.415 --> 00:03:08.121
require, like, a bolt of
lightning, like, an inspiration.

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:12.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
And my argument is that
nobody that --

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:15.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
Nobody's just walking down
the street and has no intentio

00:03:15.128 --> 00:03:18.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
to write a song is going to ge
hit by a bolt of lightning

00:03:18.331 --> 00:03:20.433 line:92%
and decide to write a song

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:22.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
You basically have to put
yourself in the path

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:28.141 align:start size:94% position:6%
of that kind of inspiration,
and I think you do that

00:03:28.141 --> 00:03:31.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
by just working at
it when you're not inspired,

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:34.547 line:92%
working at it as a daily -

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:37.617 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know,
just a practice that you have,

00:03:37.617 --> 00:03:40.753 size:78% position:39%
a habit that you have
to remind yourself

00:03:40.753 --> 00:03:42.789 align:start size:94% position:6%
to just kind of be open
to things.

00:03:42.789 --> 00:03:45.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then it happens
all the time.

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:48.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
It tends to happen almost
every day for me

00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:49.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I get
a little bit inspired,

00:03:49.963 --> 00:03:51.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, sometimes,
I get a lot inspired,

00:03:51.497 --> 00:03:56.002
but it wouldn't happen at all if
I didn't pick up the penci

00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:56.903 size:75% position:38% line:92%
or pick up a guitar.

00:03:56.903 --> 00:04:00.640 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's what I just think
everybody should do.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.443
-You mentioned that, you know,
it's important to, you know,

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:07.313
build it as a skill and make i
work and sit down and do it.

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:10.083 align:start size:94% position:6%
With that said,
are there still times

00:04:10.083 --> 00:04:13.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you think of a lyric
and you just know right away

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:19.392 line:92%
"Oh, that's really good"?

00:04:19.392 --> 00:04:20.026 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Sometimes, yeah.

00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:23.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was driving out on
the tollway the other day,

00:04:23.963 --> 00:04:29.068 size:81% position:41%
and there was a hearse
stuck at a toll plaza.

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:31.738
Like, the guy driving the hearse
didn't have any change

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:34.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they didn't have an E-ZPas
or anything like that.

00:04:34.741 --> 00:04:37.310 size:84% position:42%
And I just felt like
I was hit over the head

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.180 align:start size:94% position:6%
with the world's
greatest metaphor.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.083 line:92%
And I sang it into my phon

00:04:44.083 --> 00:04:45.952 align:start size:94% position:6%
before I even pulled over,
you know?

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:48.621 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, the classic case of
someone else's loss

00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:50.890 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
is your gain.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.159 line:92%
-Like, what a way to go, man

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:55.928 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hey, one of the things that's
always fascinating to me

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:58.998 align:start size:94% position:6%
about the Grammys
more than any other awards sho

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:02.435 align:start size:94% position:6%
is just the breadth of the kin
of artists you have

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:04.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
at a Grammys versus, you know,
just different

00:05:04.671 --> 00:05:07.840
kind of actors or, you know,
a comic and a dramatic actor

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.412 align:start size:94% position:6%
Your first Grammys
is a perfect example of this

00:05:12.412 --> 00:05:15.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
some of the people
you bumped into.

00:05:15.114 --> 00:05:21.120 size:88% position:44%
-Yeah. I think my most
notable encounter was --

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.689 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was with
a big group of people,

00:05:22.689 --> 00:05:26.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
all the Wilco guys
and wives and girlfriends.

00:05:26.359 --> 00:05:31.130
And, somehow, I ended up outside
one of the bathrooms

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:36.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
at the Staples Center holdin
everyone's program

00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:36.936 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
for the Grammys.

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:42.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
I just had a stack
of Grammy programs,

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:44.544 line:92%
and P. Diddy walked up to me

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:47.280 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he thought I was an ushe
handing out programs.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:56.055 align:start size:94% position:6%
And he tapped the programs tha
I was holding with a cane.

00:05:56.055 --> 00:05:58.057 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's like,
"Give me one of those."

00:05:58.057 --> 00:06:00.360 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm like, "No.
They're mine."

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:06.699
And it didn't become -- It
didn't escalate, thank goodness.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:13.306 size:81% position:41%
But I was also mista--
Every time I've ever

00:06:13.306 --> 00:06:18.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
gone to the Grammys, I've been
mistaken for a seat filler

00:06:18.878 --> 00:06:22.148 line:92%
And they're like, "Go! Get out!"

00:06:22.148 --> 00:06:26.319
-What I'd like to think is tha
P. Diddy both thought you were

00:06:26.319 --> 00:06:29.489 size:84% position:42%
handing out programs
and also recognized you

00:06:29.489 --> 00:06:31.958
and then said, "Do you know that
lead singer of Wilco

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:34.127 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
is also an usher?"

00:06:34.127 --> 00:06:37.597 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, that would have bee
just as plausible.

00:06:37.597 --> 00:06:40.133 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:42.402
Thanks for sticking around
to do some music for us.

00:06:42.402 --> 00:06:43.469 line:92%
And congratulations on the book.

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:45.571 align:start size:94% position:6%
-My pleasure.
Great to see you again.

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:46.305 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-Great to see you, too.

00:06:46.305 --> 00:06:48.341 size:84% position:42%
"How to Write One Song"
and "Love Is the King"

00:06:48.341 --> 00:06:49.776 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
are both out now.

